          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Design",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Variable Angle Locking Technology**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423215/images/01f13f7b-1f78-4725-9716-c5e61a5f6431_variable_angle_screw_hole.jpg",
              "caption": "Variable Angle Locking Technology"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Pangea’s variable angle locking technology uses a CoCr locking screw, which is harder than the Ti6Al4V ELI plate, allowing for the screwhead’s threads to form a definitive locking position in the plate’s locking hole by engaging the softer, Ti6Al4V ELI material.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "This technology allows the user to aim and lock the screw into the plate within a true 30° cone of the predetermined hole trajectory. The variable angle drill guide provided with the system offers guidance with respect to the limit of the 30° cone. The locking mechanism remains functional for up to three attempts at locking screw insertion.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Circular holes that accommodate screws are universal, accepting non-locking screws and locking screws within a 30° cone.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Triangular Head Shape**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423215/images/3f1f3e60-b8c6-409a-b86e-028bfddef551_triangular_head.jpg",
              "caption": "Triangular Head Shape"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Proximal surface allows for a buttressing surface and 7 screw hole options for proximal fixation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**2.0mm K-Wire Hole**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423215/images/052709de-deee-4fb5-b5c6-900e9900b634_2.0.jpg",
              "caption": "2.0mm K-Wire Hole"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "To provide temporary fixation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Placement",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "This plate is designed for proximal tibial fractures requiring plate fixation on the medial side",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "This plate is designed to be placed straight medial, but allows some anterior and posterior positioning flexibility",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Suggested for use as a single implant for medial fractures, or a supplemental medial fixation in bicondylar fractures",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Fit",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Proximal Medial Tibia Plate is designed for partial articular medial plateau fractures and used for adjunctive fixation of medial plateau fragments in bicondylar fractures.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Designed with the use of SOMA: Stryker Orthopedics Modeling and Analytics. 2",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "SOMA analysis shows the Proximal Medial Tibia plates fits closer to bone compared to Synthes LCP medial tibial plates. 3",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Indications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Pangea Tibia Plating System is indicated for the internal fixation and stabilization of tibia bone fractures and osteotomies in normal and osteopenic bone, including:",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Diaphyseal, metaphyseal, epiphyseal, extra- and intra-articular fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Non-unions, malunions, and deformities",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Periprosthetic fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Approaches",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Surgeons may use a standard medial or posteromedial approach to the proximal medial tibia",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Technical specifications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Standard plate lengths: 4-22 holes (72-324mm)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Thickness: 3.3mm",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Left and right anatomic plate options",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Drill bits: Ø2.5mm x 135mm (542020) Ø2.5mm x 215mm (542021)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
